WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.970
SPEAKER 1: Good evening,
ladies and gentlemen.

00:00:00.970 --> 00:00:01.980
Please find your seats.

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:08.940
Wow, you listened very well.

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:09.894
Thank you.

00:00:09.894 --> 00:00:11.226
[CHUCKLES]

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:13.800
Welcome to Plaster Auditorium.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:15.537
Isn't this a beautiful place?

00:00:15.537 --> 00:00:18.876
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:20.790 --> 00:00:22.850
I can't wait for you
to see the chapel.

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:26.870
If you've not gone just to stand
outside, you have to do that.

00:00:26.870 --> 00:00:28.640
It just takes your breath away.

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:31.400
Not even going inside,
just the outside part--

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:32.930
unbelievable.

00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:36.170
It is my pleasure to introduce
this afternoon's speaker, Dr.

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:37.880
Victor Davis Hanson.

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:41.810
Dr. Hanson is the Wayne and
Marcia Buske Distinguished

00:00:41.810 --> 00:00:43.780
Fellow in History at
Hillsdale College.

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:46.850
He is also a Senior Fellow
at the Hoover Institution

00:00:46.850 --> 00:00:49.670
and a professor of classics
emeritus at California State

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:51.283
University, Fresno.

00:00:51.283 --> 00:00:52.700
He earned his BA
at the University

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:55.230
of California, Santa
Cruz, and his PhD

00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:57.710
in classics from
Stanford University.

00:00:57.710 --> 00:01:01.620
In 2007, he was awarded the
National Humanities Medal.

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:04.879
And in 2008, he received
the Bradley Prize

00:01:04.879 --> 00:01:08.030
from the Lynde and Harry
Bradley Foundation.

00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:10.200
He's written for
numerous publications,

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:13.880
including the Claremont Review
of Books, The New Criterion,

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:15.893
and the Wall Street Journal.

00:01:15.893 --> 00:01:17.310
He is the author
of several books,

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:19.010
including A War Like No Other--

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:22.790
How the Athenians and Spartans
Fought the Peloponnesian War

00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:24.380
and The Second World War--

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:27.830
The First Global Conflict
Was Fought and Won.

00:01:27.830 --> 00:01:29.900
Ladies and gentlemen,
please help me warmly

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:31.790
bring up Dr. Victor
Davis Hanson.

00:01:31.790 --> 00:01:35.766
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:43.677 --> 00:01:46.010
VICTOR DAVIS HANSON: Thank
you very much for coming out.

00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:48.388
This is the 16th year
I've taught at Hillsdale.

00:01:48.388 --> 00:01:50.180
And I live on a farm
in central California.

00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:51.980
And I never tell
anybody where I'm

00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:55.520
going, so my wife said
the other day, one

00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:57.090
of our neighbors,
a farmer, said,

00:01:57.090 --> 00:01:58.595
did he go to that
weird place again?

00:01:58.595 --> 00:01:59.720
And when does he come back?

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:01.370
[LAUGHTER]

00:02:01.370 --> 00:02:04.210
So I have kind of a stealth life
here that nobody knows about.

00:02:04.210 --> 00:02:05.410
[LAUGHTER]

00:02:05.410 --> 00:02:11.310
Have you ever asked yourself, if
you were to describe yourself--

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:13.270
and can you imagine
if you said, I'm

00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:18.940
a nationalist compared to if you
said, I'm an internationalist?

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:20.560
What if your child
came home, instead

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:24.670
of saying, I major in IR,
international relations,

00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:29.080
they said, I major in NR,
nationalist relations?

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:33.040
You can imagine the
pushback you would get.

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:34.930
What I'm getting at is
"nationalism" is not

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:38.380
a good word, at least
until the last two years.

00:02:38.380 --> 00:02:40.630
That's kind of peculiar
because the word

00:02:40.630 --> 00:02:43.210
comes from the Latin "natio."

00:02:43.210 --> 00:02:45.460
And it was considered,
even in antiquity,

00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:51.100
a progressive idea that, unlike
the Greek city-state where

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:55.960
people of the same language,
roughly the same traditions,

00:02:55.960 --> 00:03:00.430
the same ethnic background
were politically fragmented,

00:03:00.430 --> 00:03:03.940
in Italy they came up with a
new concept of unification.

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:05.650
And they thought
that that would be

00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:10.810
a more equitable and successful
way of solving rivalries

00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:12.770
between different people.

00:03:12.770 --> 00:03:14.410
So what happened to the concept?

00:03:14.410 --> 00:03:17.800
Woodrow Wilson, remember,
went to Versailles

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:23.140
in January of 1919
promising that people

00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:27.970
who spoke the same language and
had the same ethnic background

00:03:27.970 --> 00:03:29.860
and mostly the
same religion would

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:31.930
get to have their
own national destiny.

00:03:31.930 --> 00:03:35.362
They would not be subject
to imperial rule by others.

00:03:35.362 --> 00:03:36.070
So what happened?

00:03:36.070 --> 00:03:38.680
I think very
briefly, the answer's

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:43.660
Germany, until the
French Revolution sort of

00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:47.080
gave a catalyst to the
modern idea of nationalism,

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:48.380
the Congress of Vienna.

00:03:48.380 --> 00:03:54.550
But the unification of Germany,
creating the largest country

00:03:54.550 --> 00:03:58.940
in Europe warped the
idea of nationalism

00:03:58.940 --> 00:04:00.710
because it was a little
bit different than

00:04:00.710 --> 00:04:02.120
the other European countries.

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:05.510
And by that, I mean it was
involved in three wars--

00:04:05.510 --> 00:04:08.000
the Franco-Prussian
War of 1871; it

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:13.130
invaded through Denmark,
Belgium, and France in 1914;

00:04:13.130 --> 00:04:17.029
and, of course, it went into
Poland in 1939 on September 1

00:04:17.029 --> 00:04:18.800
and triggered World War II.

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:22.610
But it wasn't just people felt
that Germany was aggressive,

00:04:22.610 --> 00:04:27.500
but people felt that they
had redefined nationalism.

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:31.520
By that, I mean even
before national socialism

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:34.910
of the 1930s, there was
a movement in Germany

00:04:34.910 --> 00:04:42.080
that said that Germans are
going to be defined by "blut und

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:43.160
boden"--

00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:44.480
blood and soil.

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:48.560
And what that meant was,
you could live in Germany,

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:49.940
within the boundaries
of Germany,

00:04:49.940 --> 00:04:53.420
you could speak German, you
could be a sixth generation

00:04:53.420 --> 00:04:58.070
German, you could be a
Lutheran or a Catholic,

00:04:58.070 --> 00:05:00.530
but you would not be
German if you didn't have

00:05:00.530 --> 00:05:02.840
a particular racial ancestry.

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:06.620
And that goes back to the 19th
century when writers said that.

00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:09.300
And how did that happen
in Germany and not France?

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:14.640
And the answer was that
in antiquity, Rome,

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:17.210
which was a multiracial
society like our own,

00:05:17.210 --> 00:05:20.210
had incorporated most
of Western Europe

00:05:20.210 --> 00:05:24.320
and, to some degree, northern
Europe, but not Germany.

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:26.600
The Romans felt that these
people were very fierce.

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:28.760
They were beyond the
Danube and the Rhine,

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:30.440
and they were
going to be let be.

00:05:30.440 --> 00:05:33.350
You read Tacitus' essay
on ancient Germany,

00:05:33.350 --> 00:05:37.400
"The Germania," and he says
they are very different people.

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:40.880
And what had been sort of
deprecatory in antiquity

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:45.320
was flipped upside down after
the unification of Germany,

00:05:45.320 --> 00:05:48.560
as that we were better because
we're never mongrelized.

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:51.830
And that sort of discredited
the idea of nationalism,

00:05:51.830 --> 00:05:55.910
because in Italy, Mussolini
said, we have "La Razza"

00:05:55.910 --> 00:05:56.990
with two Z's.

00:05:56.990 --> 00:06:00.200
It's kind of scary, because
that has a modern connotation.

00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:02.700
And then Franco wrote a novel--

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:06.090
General Franco in Spain-- called
La Raza, believe it or not.

00:06:06.090 --> 00:06:08.630
And they both
parroted the Nazi idea

00:06:08.630 --> 00:06:11.300
that you couldn't be
Spanish and Italian even

00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:14.660
if you spoke Italian or Spanish
and you lived here, unless you

00:06:14.660 --> 00:06:16.910
had a racial essence.

00:06:16.910 --> 00:06:20.570
And by the way, if you look
at the Constitution of Mexico

00:06:20.570 --> 00:06:23.660
today, it does say that
immigration shall not

00:06:23.660 --> 00:06:26.930
impair the racial
equilibrium of Mexico.

00:06:26.930 --> 00:06:29.900
So what I'm getting at is there
is a strain of nationalism

00:06:29.900 --> 00:06:34.960
that has polluted the otherwise
positive view of nationalism.

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:36.910
And it's too bad
because if you're

00:06:36.910 --> 00:06:40.792
going to organize people--
people are not born homogenic.

00:06:40.792 --> 00:06:42.500
There's not a homogeneity
that's natural.

00:06:42.500 --> 00:06:43.870
So if you're going to
organize different people

00:06:43.870 --> 00:06:45.670
under a political
framework, there's

00:06:45.670 --> 00:06:47.800
a limited amount of choices.

00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:50.110
You can use force--

00:06:50.110 --> 00:06:52.900
force of religion,
like the caliphate

00:06:52.900 --> 00:06:56.350
of the 14th century or the
Ottoman Empire caliphate

00:06:56.350 --> 00:06:59.680
and force people to be
Muslims of all different races

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:01.020
and nationalities.

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:03.190
Or you can be the
Soviet Union and say,

00:07:03.190 --> 00:07:05.650
you may be Chechnyan
or Ukrainian.

00:07:05.650 --> 00:07:08.470
But you're a Marxist
Leninist first of all.

00:07:08.470 --> 00:07:10.510
Or Yugoslavia-- as
long as Tito is alive

00:07:10.510 --> 00:07:13.780
and says we're all Communists,
we're not Serbs or Bosnian.

00:07:13.780 --> 00:07:17.410
If you don't use that
imperial coercion,

00:07:17.410 --> 00:07:20.560
then you can do what Wilson
thought was a liberal idea.

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:24.040
You can allow people to
create national boundaries

00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:26.110
based on their race.

00:07:26.110 --> 00:07:27.680
And you can have
several of them.

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:30.850
There can be an independent
Serbia or Bosnia.

00:07:30.850 --> 00:07:33.850
And you can have
this ethnic state.

00:07:33.850 --> 00:07:37.000
And as long as it treats its
neighbors OK and Lithuania

00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:39.490
treats Estonia OK,
there's no problem.

00:07:39.490 --> 00:07:42.640
Usually that doesn't happen
very often in history.

00:07:42.640 --> 00:07:44.260
And there's a third alternative.

00:07:44.260 --> 00:07:45.670
And it's very rare.

00:07:45.670 --> 00:07:49.030
And that is to combine
people of different races

00:07:49.030 --> 00:07:52.570
and ethnic backgrounds and
creeds into one country

00:07:52.570 --> 00:07:54.110
and not use coercion.

00:07:54.110 --> 00:07:56.440
Rome pulled it off
for a few centuries.

00:07:56.440 --> 00:07:59.680
And nobody has except us.

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:02.260
And so what unites people
in this audience that

00:08:02.260 --> 00:08:07.550
do not look like each other
is a common national story--

00:08:07.550 --> 00:08:11.380
the Constitution, the Bill
of Rights, the 4th of July,

00:08:11.380 --> 00:08:12.790
the Gettysburg Address--

00:08:12.790 --> 00:08:17.270
and the notion that
Faulkner or Steinbeck

00:08:17.270 --> 00:08:20.140
or The Last of the
Mohicans does not

00:08:20.140 --> 00:08:22.850
belong to any particular
race or creed.

00:08:22.850 --> 00:08:23.890
It's an American idea.

00:08:23.890 --> 00:08:25.850
It's the melting pot.

00:08:25.850 --> 00:08:27.900
And it has to be worked
upon all the time,

00:08:27.900 --> 00:08:30.650
because it's not a
natural phenomenon.

00:08:30.650 --> 00:08:33.490
It's a construct that
we, in the melting pot,

00:08:33.490 --> 00:08:35.710
will unite people by ideas.

00:08:35.710 --> 00:08:39.010
And that means that Americans
can look like anyone.

00:08:39.010 --> 00:08:41.710
None of you in this audience
can go to Japan and say,

00:08:41.710 --> 00:08:42.640
I'm Japanese.

00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:46.840
Even if you're legally Japanese,
you'll never be fully accepted.

00:08:46.840 --> 00:08:48.730
Same is true of China.

00:08:48.730 --> 00:08:51.700
I don't think Condoleezza Rice
could be chancellor of Germany

00:08:51.700 --> 00:08:52.860
today.

00:08:52.860 --> 00:08:54.910
I just don't think
it's going to happen.

00:08:54.910 --> 00:08:57.850
We're the only country
that's pulled this off.

00:08:57.850 --> 00:09:01.180
And the key to
this benign or good

00:09:01.180 --> 00:09:04.330
nationalism is citizenship--

00:09:04.330 --> 00:09:06.460
the idea that the American
citizen is unique.

00:09:06.460 --> 00:09:10.900
And I'm very worried because
I think citizenship is eroding

00:09:10.900 --> 00:09:12.220
and it's becoming meaningless.

00:09:12.220 --> 00:09:14.320
And with it, nationalism
is disappearing

00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:16.190
in the positive sense.

00:09:16.190 --> 00:09:20.110
And it's happening in a very
strange way at two levels.

00:09:20.110 --> 00:09:23.540
I guess we would call some of
us pre-citizens and some of us

00:09:23.540 --> 00:09:25.150
post-citizen.

00:09:25.150 --> 00:09:29.620
And what's common is we forgot
how citizenry developed.

00:09:29.620 --> 00:09:34.420
It occurred for the first
time in the seventh century

00:09:34.420 --> 00:09:35.290
in ancient Greece.

00:09:35.290 --> 00:09:37.690
And you know the word
"politeai," a citizen.

00:09:37.690 --> 00:09:41.110
You get political,
policy, even police.

00:09:41.110 --> 00:09:44.260
And it was elaborated on and
institutionalized in Rome

00:09:44.260 --> 00:09:46.935
as the "civis," the civic--

00:09:46.935 --> 00:09:51.850
a "civis," civic,
civil, civilization.

00:09:51.850 --> 00:09:55.390
And the idea is that
a particular resident

00:09:55.390 --> 00:09:58.540
within these boundaries
was not a subject of a king

00:09:58.540 --> 00:09:59.440
or dictator.

00:09:59.440 --> 00:10:03.790
And he wasn't a wild tribesman
up in Gaul or Scandinavia.

00:10:03.790 --> 00:10:06.010
He was a unique
person who could pass

00:10:06.010 --> 00:10:09.070
on property to his children.

00:10:09.070 --> 00:10:10.090
He could vote.

00:10:10.090 --> 00:10:11.440
He could make his own laws.

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:15.040
And he had the choice when and
where to live and how long.

00:10:15.040 --> 00:10:16.840
He was a free person,
in other words.

00:10:16.840 --> 00:10:18.380
That seems self-evident now.

00:10:18.380 --> 00:10:19.750
It is very rare.

00:10:19.750 --> 00:10:21.910
There was nothing like
it in the Near East,

00:10:21.910 --> 00:10:24.460
nothing like it in Sumeria
or Egypt, nothing like it

00:10:24.460 --> 00:10:26.480
in tribal northern Europe.

00:10:26.480 --> 00:10:27.820
It was a unique idea.

00:10:27.820 --> 00:10:29.290
Nothing like it
in the New World--

00:10:29.290 --> 00:10:32.530
Aztecs, no, Mayans, no.

00:10:32.530 --> 00:10:35.380
But now what we're trying to
do, I guess in this country,

00:10:35.380 --> 00:10:38.680
either by laxity or by
intent, is revert back

00:10:38.680 --> 00:10:39.880
to pre-citizenship.

00:10:39.880 --> 00:10:41.780
And it occurs it
at three levels.

00:10:41.780 --> 00:10:46.180
The first is, we're blurring
the line between a mere resident

00:10:46.180 --> 00:10:47.110
and a citizen.

00:10:47.110 --> 00:10:49.870
We have about 60
million residents

00:10:49.870 --> 00:10:51.130
in the United States--

00:10:51.130 --> 00:10:53.050
largest number we've ever had--

00:10:53.050 --> 00:10:55.400
that are not US citizens.

00:10:55.400 --> 00:10:57.070
There's no problem
with 40 million

00:10:57.070 --> 00:10:59.420
because they have green
cards and they're legal,

00:10:59.420 --> 00:11:01.330
even though that's
a large number

00:11:01.330 --> 00:11:03.970
to assimilate, intermarry,
and integrate into the body

00:11:03.970 --> 00:11:04.897
politic--

00:11:04.897 --> 00:11:05.980
something we should watch.

00:11:05.980 --> 00:11:09.010
What we have, according
to the Yale and MIT

00:11:09.010 --> 00:11:12.970
latest study, about 20 million
people who are here illegally.

00:11:12.970 --> 00:11:15.760
And the strange
thing about it is

00:11:15.760 --> 00:11:19.840
that the chief ingredients of
American citizenship that said,

00:11:19.840 --> 00:11:22.720
an American citizen, and
only American citizen,

00:11:22.720 --> 00:11:26.040
can leave or enter the
country as he pleases

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:30.820
or vote in an election as he
wishes or stay indefinitely

00:11:30.820 --> 00:11:33.790
in the United States
due to his legal rights,

00:11:33.790 --> 00:11:35.240
has been completely blurred.

00:11:35.240 --> 00:11:38.030
Where I live in California,
or if you're here illegally,

00:11:38.030 --> 00:11:41.360
you can go back across
the border as you wish.

00:11:41.360 --> 00:11:43.460
In San Francisco, if
you're here illegally,

00:11:43.460 --> 00:11:46.130
you can vote in school
board elections.

00:11:46.130 --> 00:11:49.290
And the same is true in
some places in New England.

00:11:49.290 --> 00:11:51.590
And as we learned with the
DACA and DREAMer program,

00:11:51.590 --> 00:11:54.900
you can live here indefinitely.

00:11:54.900 --> 00:11:57.860
And it even is a
little bit more extreme

00:11:57.860 --> 00:12:00.950
than just equality
with citizenship.

00:12:00.950 --> 00:12:06.260
Sometimes it's mere
discrimination.

00:12:06.260 --> 00:12:10.160
A person in California who
is charged with a crime,

00:12:10.160 --> 00:12:12.950
if the person is a resident
rather than a citizen,

00:12:12.950 --> 00:12:15.890
is not subject to a
federal immigration law

00:12:15.890 --> 00:12:17.460
to the full extent.

00:12:17.460 --> 00:12:20.140
If you or I come into the
Detroit Airport from overseas

00:12:20.140 --> 00:12:23.900
and we do not have a passport,
we're going to be detained.

00:12:23.900 --> 00:12:29.510
If you are in Fresno county
and 500 other sanctuary city

00:12:29.510 --> 00:12:31.225
jurisdictions and
you commit a crime

00:12:31.225 --> 00:12:32.600
and you're here
illegally, you're

00:12:32.600 --> 00:12:34.850
not going to be deported.

00:12:34.850 --> 00:12:35.970
It's not going to happen.

00:12:35.970 --> 00:12:39.170
You're given a
privilege, as it is.

00:12:39.170 --> 00:12:42.290
If you're in California
and you are here illegally,

00:12:42.290 --> 00:12:47.000
you're going to get a
license, just like a citizen--

00:12:47.000 --> 00:12:49.220
but not quite just
like a citizen,

00:12:49.220 --> 00:12:51.980
because a federal law
says for you to travel,

00:12:51.980 --> 00:12:54.560
you have to have
proof of citizenship

00:12:54.560 --> 00:12:59.010
to get a proper ID for
the federal transportation

00:12:59.010 --> 00:12:59.510
authority.

00:12:59.510 --> 00:13:02.000
So that means that all
the citizens in California

00:13:02.000 --> 00:13:04.760
have to turn in their
driver's licenses next year

00:13:04.760 --> 00:13:07.012
and reapply with a passport.

00:13:07.012 --> 00:13:08.720
In other words, they
have an extra burden

00:13:08.720 --> 00:13:12.230
to pay because residents had
an advantage of not having

00:13:12.230 --> 00:13:17.090
to have the same type of ID
for their version of a driver's

00:13:17.090 --> 00:13:18.230
license.

00:13:18.230 --> 00:13:20.540
So in some sense, in
many of our states,

00:13:20.540 --> 00:13:23.768
it doesn't really matter whether
you're a citizen or resident.

00:13:23.768 --> 00:13:25.310
And you can see
where that's leading.

00:13:25.310 --> 00:13:27.020
People who come from
different countries

00:13:27.020 --> 00:13:30.440
and are here illegally,
they have no investment

00:13:30.440 --> 00:13:34.950
to the same degree as we do in
our constitutional documents.

00:13:34.950 --> 00:13:37.370
They don't really want to know
part of the national story.

00:13:37.370 --> 00:13:40.340
Where I live, candidates
from south of the border

00:13:40.340 --> 00:13:43.610
fly into the Central Valley
of California and campaign.

00:13:43.610 --> 00:13:44.690
And people vote for them.

00:13:44.690 --> 00:13:46.940
And they vote in
the United States.

00:13:46.940 --> 00:13:49.340
You can see the problem
of a fragmenting society,

00:13:49.340 --> 00:13:51.230
because what the
theme is, that we're

00:13:51.230 --> 00:13:54.313
of various different races
and ethnic backgrounds.

00:13:54.313 --> 00:13:55.730
And this is not
normal in history,

00:13:55.730 --> 00:13:57.130
and we have to make
the extra effort.

00:13:57.130 --> 00:13:58.713
But we're not making
the extra effort.

00:13:58.713 --> 00:14:00.380
We're doing just the opposite.

00:14:00.380 --> 00:14:04.100
But besides turning into a
country of mere residents,

00:14:04.100 --> 00:14:08.180
we're also becoming
a country of tribes.

00:14:08.180 --> 00:14:10.880
And by that, I mean the
idea of multiracialism,

00:14:10.880 --> 00:14:12.630
that we're all of
different races

00:14:12.630 --> 00:14:15.620
but we share a common
culture is eroding.

00:14:15.620 --> 00:14:19.970
And now we're becoming many
cultures and many races.

00:14:19.970 --> 00:14:25.280
Stanford University, you can
determine the racial background

00:14:25.280 --> 00:14:27.110
of your roommate
before you enroll.

00:14:27.110 --> 00:14:29.270
The same is true at
Claremont College.

00:14:29.270 --> 00:14:31.550
There are places on
university campuses called

00:14:31.550 --> 00:14:34.840
"safe spaces" that are
reserved for particular people

00:14:34.840 --> 00:14:36.740
and of a particular race.

00:14:36.740 --> 00:14:39.860
There are theme houses and
dorms where people segregate

00:14:39.860 --> 00:14:44.060
according to their race.

00:14:44.060 --> 00:14:46.890
Did anybody ask
yourself why, for years,

00:14:46.890 --> 00:14:48.650
Elizabeth Warren and
other politicians

00:14:48.650 --> 00:14:52.510
had railed against
white privilege.

00:14:52.510 --> 00:14:55.690
If that were true, why
would Elizabeth Warren

00:14:55.690 --> 00:14:57.070
choose to be a Native American?

00:14:57.070 --> 00:15:00.430
In other words, culturally
appropriate another identity

00:15:00.430 --> 00:15:02.200
which, according to
her own logic, would

00:15:02.200 --> 00:15:05.260
put her at a disadvantage,
giving the ubiquity

00:15:05.260 --> 00:15:06.540
of white privilege.

00:15:06.540 --> 00:15:09.460
And the answer is that we have
returned to the "one-drop"

00:15:09.460 --> 00:15:11.620
rule of the Old South--

00:15:11.620 --> 00:15:15.640
that one drop, 1/16, allows
you to become in a minority.

00:15:15.640 --> 00:15:18.380
So the reason that she said
she was a Cherokee person--

00:15:18.380 --> 00:15:20.020
remember, the
Cherokee nation-- is

00:15:20.020 --> 00:15:23.860
she wanted a careerist advantage
over someone else who chose

00:15:23.860 --> 00:15:25.480
not to play that game.

00:15:25.480 --> 00:15:28.450
And Harvard was only happy
to comply because the law

00:15:28.450 --> 00:15:31.750
school bragged that she was
their first minority Native

00:15:31.750 --> 00:15:34.110
American faculty member.

00:15:34.110 --> 00:15:39.160
So what you can see is a
retribalization of America

00:15:39.160 --> 00:15:40.510
at the expense of citizenship.

00:15:40.510 --> 00:15:45.970
I went to a grammar school that
was about 90% Mexican-American.

00:15:45.970 --> 00:15:48.970
And maybe it was
culturally insensitive,

00:15:48.970 --> 00:15:51.490
maybe the melting
pot got too hot,

00:15:51.490 --> 00:15:54.800
but we took people from
all over Latin America

00:15:54.800 --> 00:15:57.400
and we made them into
American citizens.

00:15:57.400 --> 00:15:58.420
And I've noticed--

00:15:58.420 --> 00:16:00.820
I'm 66-- that when
I see some people

00:16:00.820 --> 00:16:03.670
that I went to kindergarten
and first grade,

00:16:03.670 --> 00:16:06.370
that they went
from Juan to Johns,

00:16:06.370 --> 00:16:08.440
and they dropped the
accent on their last name.

00:16:08.440 --> 00:16:11.590
Now in their 60s, they're
back to Juan again.

00:16:11.590 --> 00:16:14.507
And they've added accents and
hyphenations to their name.

00:16:14.507 --> 00:16:16.090
And what would be
the purpose of that?

00:16:16.090 --> 00:16:19.240
The purpose is they get
a general impression

00:16:19.240 --> 00:16:20.880
from the society
at large that there

00:16:20.880 --> 00:16:25.600
is an advantage not to identify
just as a plain, old American--

00:16:25.600 --> 00:16:28.600
that you have to be a member
of a particular tribe--

00:16:28.600 --> 00:16:31.820
the LGBT community, the
Asian-American community,

00:16:31.820 --> 00:16:33.400
the black community.

00:16:33.400 --> 00:16:35.410
And you can see
where this is going.

00:16:35.410 --> 00:16:38.560
70% of the country
self-identifies as white.

00:16:38.560 --> 00:16:40.030
And the last thing
you would want

00:16:40.030 --> 00:16:43.810
would be 70% of the country
to start identifying as white.

00:16:43.810 --> 00:16:46.180
It would be the worst thing
in the world to happen.

00:16:46.180 --> 00:16:48.010
But that's the
logic of tribalism

00:16:48.010 --> 00:16:52.930
that leads to something like
Rwanda or the former Yugoslavia

00:16:52.930 --> 00:16:55.210
or what we saw in Iraq.

00:16:55.210 --> 00:16:59.050
So we have to be careful
of people blurring

00:16:59.050 --> 00:17:01.930
the line between a
citizen and a resident,

00:17:01.930 --> 00:17:06.045
and an American and
a tribal member.

00:17:06.045 --> 00:17:07.420
There's another
phenomenon that's

00:17:07.420 --> 00:17:13.700
just as scary that's going on in
this theme of pre-citizenship.

00:17:13.700 --> 00:17:17.599
America was based on the
viability of a middle class.

00:17:17.599 --> 00:17:19.940
If you read the essays
of Jefferson, he said,

00:17:19.940 --> 00:17:22.819
when we pile up
everybody into the cities

00:17:22.819 --> 00:17:24.950
and they're no
longer farm owners,

00:17:24.950 --> 00:17:26.359
then we'll have two classes--

00:17:26.359 --> 00:17:28.010
wealthy and poor.

00:17:28.010 --> 00:17:31.850
If you read St. John de
Crevecoeur or you read Alexis

00:17:31.850 --> 00:17:35.120
de Tocqueville, the
theme is the same,

00:17:35.120 --> 00:17:39.710
that a solid, property-owning
middle class has advantages

00:17:39.710 --> 00:17:42.780
over the poor because they're
invested in a particular place,

00:17:42.780 --> 00:17:46.190
they showed a sobriety of
judgment to save their money,

00:17:46.190 --> 00:17:48.770
they're stationary people,
they're sober and judicious,

00:17:48.770 --> 00:17:51.920
they do not look to government
to the same degree as the poor.

00:17:51.920 --> 00:17:54.620
And unlike the wealthy,
they don't have the power

00:17:54.620 --> 00:17:57.200
or leverage to
affect government,

00:17:57.200 --> 00:17:59.570
to act in the interests
of a small clique.

00:17:59.570 --> 00:18:01.400
So there was this romance.

00:18:01.400 --> 00:18:04.280
And it was easy to do because
90% of the country, as I said,

00:18:04.280 --> 00:18:05.780
were agrarians.

00:18:05.780 --> 00:18:08.030
We survived the
Industrial Revolution--

00:18:08.030 --> 00:18:10.490
the middle class
did, I should say--

00:18:10.490 --> 00:18:13.310
and it was transmogrified
into the American ideal

00:18:13.310 --> 00:18:18.020
that everybody would own a home
and they would have a job that

00:18:18.020 --> 00:18:21.250
would open a pathway
to upward mobility

00:18:21.250 --> 00:18:23.000
and they'd have an
avenue to go to college

00:18:23.000 --> 00:18:24.350
for self-improvement.

00:18:24.350 --> 00:18:25.910
And out of that
matrix, they would

00:18:25.910 --> 00:18:28.430
be independent and
autonomous people.

00:18:28.430 --> 00:18:31.520
And all of us in the
middle class, then,

00:18:31.520 --> 00:18:34.670
could tell the poor, we're not
going to redistribute money.

00:18:34.670 --> 00:18:36.410
And we could also
tell the very wealthy,

00:18:36.410 --> 00:18:40.360
and you're not going to be
a crony inside-capitalist.

00:18:40.360 --> 00:18:43.630
What's happened, though,
we've psychologically

00:18:43.630 --> 00:18:45.310
deprecated the middle class.

00:18:45.310 --> 00:18:47.440
Our elites say that
the middle class

00:18:47.440 --> 00:18:49.970
lacks the culture
of the wealthy,

00:18:49.970 --> 00:18:54.700
with our snowmobiles and SUVs
and jet skis and Winnebagos.

00:18:54.700 --> 00:18:57.930
And we don't have the
romance of the distant poor.

00:18:57.930 --> 00:19:00.940
And that reflects something
that, in actual terms,

00:19:00.940 --> 00:19:02.980
the middle class is threatened.

00:19:02.980 --> 00:19:06.820
Homeownership peaked right
before the collapse in 2008

00:19:06.820 --> 00:19:08.050
at 71%.

00:19:08.050 --> 00:19:10.282
It's down to about 62%.

00:19:10.282 --> 00:19:12.490
But unfortunately, that
doesn't tell the whole story.

00:19:12.490 --> 00:19:16.870
The percentage of a family
budget that goes to housing

00:19:16.870 --> 00:19:22.750
has gone from about 14%
in the 1950s to 44%.

00:19:22.750 --> 00:19:25.480
So people, to the degree
that they can buy a house,

00:19:25.480 --> 00:19:28.210
are spending nearly
half their income.

00:19:28.210 --> 00:19:33.430
Wages, until two years ago,
had been frozen for 10 years.

00:19:33.430 --> 00:19:36.130
The middle class didn't really
see an increase of their wages

00:19:36.130 --> 00:19:37.000
for ten years.

00:19:37.000 --> 00:19:38.900
It's gone up 3% under Trump.

00:19:38.900 --> 00:19:41.290
But until then, it
had not gone up.

00:19:41.290 --> 00:19:43.960
And when you talk about college
and upward mobility-- and this

00:19:43.960 --> 00:19:47.860
is a very apropos topic
here at Hillsdale,

00:19:47.860 --> 00:19:50.800
because the purpose
of this wonderful gala

00:19:50.800 --> 00:19:53.950
is to raise money to help
defray the cost of tuition--

00:19:53.950 --> 00:20:01.640
but we have an aggregate $1.6
trillion in student debt.

00:20:01.640 --> 00:20:04.450
I don't think we're getting
much return from most colleges,

00:20:04.450 --> 00:20:07.150
if you look at the
level of education

00:20:07.150 --> 00:20:09.520
that a recent graduate shows.

00:20:09.520 --> 00:20:12.250
But more importantly, there
were certain attributes

00:20:12.250 --> 00:20:14.500
about the middle class
that allowed citizens

00:20:14.500 --> 00:20:17.860
to be traditional and
to be participants

00:20:17.860 --> 00:20:19.060
in the American story.

00:20:19.060 --> 00:20:23.170
And one was, of course, people
got married quite early--

00:20:23.170 --> 00:20:23.930
in their 20s.

00:20:23.930 --> 00:20:26.500
The average age
until 1952 was 22.

00:20:26.500 --> 00:20:29.590
Some of you got
married at 21 or 22.

00:20:29.590 --> 00:20:33.580
Until just 2000, the
average number of children

00:20:33.580 --> 00:20:34.860
was about 2.1.

00:20:34.860 --> 00:20:36.880
It's now about 1.7.

00:20:36.880 --> 00:20:40.300
People are on average getting
married at about 29 now.

00:20:40.300 --> 00:20:43.420
I mentioned housing going
down and middle class wages.

00:20:43.420 --> 00:20:48.100
And student debt,
about 5% of students

00:20:48.100 --> 00:20:51.350
had student debt in the '50s.

00:20:51.350 --> 00:20:52.660
And so think what we're doing.

00:20:52.660 --> 00:20:55.870
We're telling young
people, you're really

00:20:55.870 --> 00:20:57.910
not going to be
able to buy a house

00:20:57.910 --> 00:21:01.480
and you're an indentured
servant to your former college

00:21:01.480 --> 00:21:04.780
that you have this
$200,000 debt.

00:21:04.780 --> 00:21:07.420
And they say, I can't
afford to get married.

00:21:07.420 --> 00:21:09.220
I'm not going to have children.

00:21:09.220 --> 00:21:10.580
I can't go to the suburbs.

00:21:10.580 --> 00:21:12.100
I don't want to live in
a town like Hillsdale.

00:21:12.100 --> 00:21:13.392
I don't want to live in a farm.

00:21:13.392 --> 00:21:15.940
I want to be a hipster
in a urban environment.

00:21:15.940 --> 00:21:17.740
And that's pretty
much the ingredients

00:21:17.740 --> 00:21:20.110
of what you see on
the news with Antifa.

00:21:20.110 --> 00:21:21.790
That's the profile.

00:21:21.790 --> 00:21:24.070
So the destruction
of the middle class

00:21:24.070 --> 00:21:26.410
then turns you into
a nation of peasants,

00:21:26.410 --> 00:21:29.962
as we see in California.

00:21:29.962 --> 00:21:31.420
One out of five--
and we're getting

00:21:31.420 --> 00:21:34.090
close to one out of four--
lives below the poverty line.

00:21:34.090 --> 00:21:35.800
And yet, we have
the largest number

00:21:35.800 --> 00:21:40.840
of zip codes of people who were
in the most affluent counties

00:21:40.840 --> 00:21:41.680
in the country.

00:21:41.680 --> 00:21:43.660
We have the highest
number of billionaires.

00:21:43.660 --> 00:21:46.630
But if you drive up to Stanford
University on any given day,

00:21:46.630 --> 00:21:48.790
you'll see people living
in Winnebagos right out

00:21:48.790 --> 00:21:50.230
in front of campus--

00:21:50.230 --> 00:21:51.560
can't afford to buy a home.

00:21:51.560 --> 00:21:55.750
And they're working for Google
or Facebook or Yahoo or Apple

00:21:55.750 --> 00:21:59.690
with a market capitalization
of $4 trillion.

00:21:59.690 --> 00:22:02.290
I submit to you that
that's not a good way

00:22:02.290 --> 00:22:07.360
to create a stable American
nationalist patriotic country,

00:22:07.360 --> 00:22:10.150
when you're destroying
the middle class.

00:22:10.150 --> 00:22:15.070
So we don't want to be mere
residents or tribespeople

00:22:15.070 --> 00:22:16.840
or peasantry.

00:22:16.840 --> 00:22:21.940
But I'd like to suggest that,
on the other end of the scale,

00:22:21.940 --> 00:22:26.440
on the elite side, there's just
as much danger to nationalism

00:22:26.440 --> 00:22:28.740
and citizenship.

00:22:28.740 --> 00:22:31.160
Our elite tend to
believe on the coasts

00:22:31.160 --> 00:22:33.640
that they're not citizens
so much of the United States

00:22:33.640 --> 00:22:36.250
as citizens of the world.

00:22:36.250 --> 00:22:38.080
They like to be called
"internationalists"

00:22:38.080 --> 00:22:38.870
or "globalists."

00:22:38.870 --> 00:22:41.290
They do not want to be
known as "nationalists."

00:22:41.290 --> 00:22:45.160
They have more faith
in the Paris Climate

00:22:45.160 --> 00:22:47.770
Accord or the Kyoto
Agreements than they

00:22:47.770 --> 00:22:49.210
do their own government.

00:22:49.210 --> 00:22:50.920
They're ashamed of
the Second Amendment

00:22:50.920 --> 00:22:52.610
when they go to Europe.

00:22:52.610 --> 00:22:55.090
They feel that
trans-Pacific trade

00:22:55.090 --> 00:22:59.988
is much better than negotiating
individual trade deals.

00:22:59.988 --> 00:23:01.030
They love the World Bank.

00:23:01.030 --> 00:23:02.800
They love the International
Monetary Fund.

00:23:02.800 --> 00:23:06.490
They think the UN Commission
on Civil Rights or Human Rights

00:23:06.490 --> 00:23:10.480
has more authority than does
the US House of Representatives.

00:23:10.480 --> 00:23:13.180
And it's an old idea--

00:23:13.180 --> 00:23:16.150
out of that, comes
a transnationalism.

00:23:16.150 --> 00:23:21.580
That, just as globalization took
American free market capitalism

00:23:21.580 --> 00:23:24.070
and spread it around
to 7 billion people

00:23:24.070 --> 00:23:27.100
and gave us instant
communications--

00:23:27.100 --> 00:23:28.960
instant travel, almost.

00:23:28.960 --> 00:23:30.520
Anybody can get on
a phone and call

00:23:30.520 --> 00:23:32.500
any of the other 7 billion
people in the world

00:23:32.500 --> 00:23:34.820
almost quite easily.

00:23:34.820 --> 00:23:38.290
But what did not follow
was American constitutional

00:23:38.290 --> 00:23:38.850
government.

00:23:38.850 --> 00:23:40.540
Most people said,
that is too strange.

00:23:40.540 --> 00:23:41.720
It's too weird.

00:23:41.720 --> 00:23:43.210
We don't want it.

00:23:43.210 --> 00:23:46.090
And by that, they meant
they don't like the Second

00:23:46.090 --> 00:23:48.970
Amendment-- "they" being
Western Europeans and most

00:23:48.970 --> 00:23:50.150
of the world.

00:23:50.150 --> 00:23:52.810
They have no problem
with late-term abortion.

00:23:52.810 --> 00:23:56.710
They feel the government should
intervene and restructure

00:23:56.710 --> 00:23:58.750
radically the
economy on the theory

00:23:58.750 --> 00:24:02.020
of man-made global warming.

00:24:02.020 --> 00:24:06.980
And so there's a lot of ideas
out in the globalized world

00:24:06.980 --> 00:24:09.230
that are antithetical
to America's.

00:24:09.230 --> 00:24:11.570
And we have a lot of
people in the country who,

00:24:11.570 --> 00:24:13.310
in the Progressive
movement, feel

00:24:13.310 --> 00:24:17.330
that we have to move beyond
Americanism and join the world,

00:24:17.330 --> 00:24:20.820
just as we have
economically and culturally.

00:24:20.820 --> 00:24:22.460
There's a second
thing that's happening

00:24:22.460 --> 00:24:24.630
with post-citizenship.

00:24:24.630 --> 00:24:27.680
And that's a formal assault
on the US Constitution

00:24:27.680 --> 00:24:29.440
by the elite classes.

00:24:29.440 --> 00:24:34.640
And by that, I mean we've now
had a second election in which,

00:24:34.640 --> 00:24:39.560
as the framers envisioned, from
time to time, the candidate who

00:24:39.560 --> 00:24:44.270
won the popular vote might
not win the electoral college.

00:24:44.270 --> 00:24:46.820
It happened with George
W. Bush and Al Gore.

00:24:46.820 --> 00:24:49.410
And it happened with Hillary
Clinton and Donald Trump.

00:24:49.410 --> 00:24:54.080
Unfortunately, in both
cases, Progressives lost.

00:24:54.080 --> 00:24:58.250
And given the Trump trajectory,
people are very angry.

00:24:58.250 --> 00:25:00.830
And they think the answer
to it is to change either

00:25:00.830 --> 00:25:03.290
the Constitution or
the accepted custom

00:25:03.290 --> 00:25:05.090
and practice of
the last 100 years.

00:25:05.090 --> 00:25:07.280
We mentioned sanctuary cities.

00:25:07.280 --> 00:25:09.350
I would just say
that nullification

00:25:09.350 --> 00:25:12.320
is a very common creed
now in the United States.

00:25:12.320 --> 00:25:13.640
But it only goes one way.

00:25:13.640 --> 00:25:15.620
You can nullify federal
immigration law.

00:25:15.620 --> 00:25:19.070
But believe me, if the
people of Provo, Utah,

00:25:19.070 --> 00:25:21.560
decide that within
their jurisdiction,

00:25:21.560 --> 00:25:24.410
they want to nullify federal
handgun registration,

00:25:24.410 --> 00:25:26.180
they're not going
to be able to do it.

00:25:26.180 --> 00:25:30.950
If people in Bakersfield say the
Endangered Species Act does not

00:25:30.950 --> 00:25:34.410
apply in Kern County, they're
not going to be able to do it.

00:25:34.410 --> 00:25:37.160
It's a one-sided,
asymmetrical view,

00:25:37.160 --> 00:25:40.970
sort of like South
Carolina in 1832 and 1833,

00:25:40.970 --> 00:25:42.680
when they nullified
federal law and said

00:25:42.680 --> 00:25:45.180
tariff policy doesn't apply.

00:25:45.180 --> 00:25:48.920
It's a road to perdition,
as we know from 1861.

00:25:48.920 --> 00:25:50.720
And yet it's getting
more and more common

00:25:50.720 --> 00:25:54.670
that a particular
jurisdiction can defy,

00:25:54.670 --> 00:25:57.710
and legally so, the
US constitution.

00:25:57.710 --> 00:26:00.800
People want to return
to FDR's half-baked

00:26:00.800 --> 00:26:02.750
plan of packing the court.

00:26:02.750 --> 00:26:04.877
I don't know if
they think they're

00:26:04.877 --> 00:26:05.960
going to get away with it.

00:26:05.960 --> 00:26:06.860
FDR didn't.

00:26:06.860 --> 00:26:08.780
But if you look
at court decisions

00:26:08.780 --> 00:26:12.890
after the court-packing
scheme of the late '30s,

00:26:12.890 --> 00:26:14.900
you can make the
argument that justices

00:26:14.900 --> 00:26:17.840
tend to be a little bit more
progressive in fear that they

00:26:17.840 --> 00:26:22.670
might either have more justices
or face mandatory retirement.

00:26:22.670 --> 00:26:24.603
So there is an effort,
at least, to suggest

00:26:24.603 --> 00:26:26.520
we're going to change
the nature of the court.

00:26:26.520 --> 00:26:28.880
And even if we can't,
that's a message to you

00:26:28.880 --> 00:26:32.360
conservative people
to shape up or else.

00:26:32.360 --> 00:26:34.310
There's an idea
that, as you know,

00:26:34.310 --> 00:26:36.980
every single candidate that
I know of, that I've heard--

00:26:36.980 --> 00:26:38.300
and I checked today--

00:26:38.300 --> 00:26:42.183
is in favor of abolishing
the electoral college.

00:26:42.183 --> 00:26:43.850
People don't understand
what it was for.

00:26:43.850 --> 00:26:46.700
It was for to give equal
representation to people

00:26:46.700 --> 00:26:52.760
outside big cities and follow
the Republican model of Rome

00:26:52.760 --> 00:26:57.680
and not the quite scary "51%
get what they want in any given

00:26:57.680 --> 00:27:00.380
day" plan of ancient Athens.

00:27:00.380 --> 00:27:03.830
And yet, that's very common.

00:27:03.830 --> 00:27:06.510
In preparation for this talk,
I did a lot of research.

00:27:06.510 --> 00:27:10.370
I didn't realize how common
it is, the progressive anger

00:27:10.370 --> 00:27:12.170
at the US Senate.

00:27:12.170 --> 00:27:15.590
And a common refrain is,
the people of Wyoming

00:27:15.590 --> 00:27:20.360
have two senators-- each
represents 245,000 people.

00:27:20.360 --> 00:27:22.340
We in California
have two senators--

00:27:22.340 --> 00:27:25.040
each represents
20 million people.

00:27:25.040 --> 00:27:25.970
That's not fair.

00:27:25.970 --> 00:27:30.620
Let's make senators
proportioned on the population

00:27:30.620 --> 00:27:32.310
as House members.

00:27:32.310 --> 00:27:34.910
And I think we'll
see some of that.

00:27:34.910 --> 00:27:38.540
In short, there's a formal
assault on the Constitution

00:27:38.540 --> 00:27:41.720
because it hasn't given
the expected results.

00:27:41.720 --> 00:27:45.710
And because the aims of the
Left are so exalted and noble,

00:27:45.710 --> 00:27:49.190
they feel that any means
necessary are justified.

00:27:49.190 --> 00:27:53.420
And finally, there's
an informal effort

00:27:53.420 --> 00:27:56.690
to create something like a
post-citizen that doesn't have

00:27:56.690 --> 00:27:58.460
to abide by the Constitution.

00:27:58.460 --> 00:28:00.650
Have you noticed we still
have a Second Amendment.

00:28:00.650 --> 00:28:03.192
But what does it mean if you're
in a rural part of the United

00:28:03.192 --> 00:28:07.040
States and you go to Walmart and
they can't sell you ammunition?

00:28:07.040 --> 00:28:09.980
In other words, social
justice warriors,

00:28:09.980 --> 00:28:12.750
the cancel culture,
social media,

00:28:12.750 --> 00:28:15.530
Hollywood, the foundations,
the universities--

00:28:15.530 --> 00:28:18.770
all of them can put
such pressure on people

00:28:18.770 --> 00:28:22.370
that they can't fully exercise
their constitutional rights,

00:28:22.370 --> 00:28:26.570
even though their rights de
jure are still in existence.

00:28:26.570 --> 00:28:28.070
If you're a cake
maker and you don't

00:28:28.070 --> 00:28:31.430
want a particular theme
of a sexual orientation,

00:28:31.430 --> 00:28:33.740
you can be coerced.

00:28:33.740 --> 00:28:35.810
If you're Carter Page
and you're innocent,

00:28:35.810 --> 00:28:38.420
but you represent an
unattractive candidate

00:28:38.420 --> 00:28:42.920
or campaign, you can be
leveraged by a prosecutor.

00:28:42.920 --> 00:28:46.430
Or if you're Michael Flynn,
you can be pled guilty.

00:28:46.430 --> 00:28:51.360
90% of all federal
prosecutors win their cases--

00:28:51.360 --> 00:28:54.140
all cases that federal
prosecutors do.

00:28:54.140 --> 00:28:56.990
So we have to be very
careful that we're not

00:28:56.990 --> 00:28:59.270
creating a situation,
while we still

00:28:59.270 --> 00:29:02.570
have these personal freedoms
protected by the Constitution,

00:29:02.570 --> 00:29:04.510
we can't exercise them.

00:29:04.510 --> 00:29:07.780
I know as a professor
and fellow at Stanford,

00:29:07.780 --> 00:29:09.580
I speak to a lot of students.

00:29:09.580 --> 00:29:12.130
And I will guarantee you
that if you were students now

00:29:12.130 --> 00:29:16.630
at Stanford, I would not say
the following because I wouldn't

00:29:16.630 --> 00:29:18.490
get to the end of the speech.

00:29:18.490 --> 00:29:21.280
If I said, I'm very
skeptical about abortion,

00:29:21.280 --> 00:29:25.360
but I especially do not like
the idea of 8,000 instances

00:29:25.360 --> 00:29:26.290
of infanticide.

00:29:26.290 --> 00:29:28.480
We've got to stop it.

00:29:28.480 --> 00:29:30.370
Half would walk out at that.

00:29:30.370 --> 00:29:32.410
The other half would
come to the podium.

00:29:32.410 --> 00:29:35.990
And who didn't come the
podium would come when I said,

00:29:35.990 --> 00:29:37.600
I do believe that
climate changes,

00:29:37.600 --> 00:29:40.660
but I either don't believe
that we're responsible for it,

00:29:40.660 --> 00:29:42.970
or if we were
responsible it justifies

00:29:42.970 --> 00:29:46.060
the radical transformations
in the economy

00:29:46.060 --> 00:29:47.230
that people are calling for.

00:29:47.230 --> 00:29:50.480
I don't think I could
finish that either.

00:29:50.480 --> 00:29:53.270
And if I said I
live in a farm house

00:29:53.270 --> 00:29:55.180
where I inherited
12 different guns.

00:29:55.180 --> 00:29:56.545
I'm very proud of them.

00:29:56.545 --> 00:29:57.920
I don't think that
would go over.

00:29:57.920 --> 00:29:59.810
In other words, there's
a freedom of speech.

00:29:59.810 --> 00:30:03.810
But if you were to try to say
that, you wouldn't be able to.

00:30:03.810 --> 00:30:06.310
But if you don't believe me,
if you all put on red MAGA hats

00:30:06.310 --> 00:30:08.480
and walk into Detroit,
see what happens to you.

00:30:08.480 --> 00:30:09.710
[LAUGHTER]

00:30:09.710 --> 00:30:12.040
And so there's
cases where we have

00:30:12.040 --> 00:30:14.500
these constitutional freedoms.

00:30:14.500 --> 00:30:16.270
But a minority of
the population that

00:30:16.270 --> 00:30:19.270
has so much more
influence and resources

00:30:19.270 --> 00:30:21.460
is able to create
a culture of shame

00:30:21.460 --> 00:30:24.040
where we don't
dare exercise them.

00:30:24.040 --> 00:30:26.950
And then de facto, it's almost
the same result as the people

00:30:26.950 --> 00:30:31.650
formally and legally trying to
invalidate the Constitution.

00:30:31.650 --> 00:30:34.970
Or if people don't want to
go out and vote in California

00:30:34.970 --> 00:30:36.720
because they're afraid
to go to the polls,

00:30:36.720 --> 00:30:39.640
it has the same effect as
a federal judge overturning

00:30:39.640 --> 00:30:43.360
a plebiscite that happened in
two cases with gay marriage.

00:30:43.360 --> 00:30:46.990
Let me just finish by
saying, what causes this?

00:30:46.990 --> 00:30:49.950
You talk to people
overseas in Asia or Africa.

00:30:49.950 --> 00:30:52.930
And they call it the
"Western disease."

00:30:52.930 --> 00:30:57.125
And what they're referring to
is a clearly defined mentality

00:30:57.125 --> 00:30:58.000
in the ancient world.

00:30:58.000 --> 00:31:00.460
You can read it in
Plato and Aristotle.

00:31:00.460 --> 00:31:03.850
You can see it especially in
Roman nihilistic literature--

00:31:03.850 --> 00:31:06.220
Tacitus, Patroneas,
[INAUDIBLE]----

00:31:06.220 --> 00:31:09.400
that suggests that
too much affluence,

00:31:09.400 --> 00:31:11.960
too much leisure creates
a laxity, a "luxus,"

00:31:11.960 --> 00:31:14.170
as the Romans called it,
where if you don't have

00:31:14.170 --> 00:31:18.070
an existential threat, then
perhaps you have the margin

00:31:18.070 --> 00:31:21.910
of error to indulge in
ideas that are detrimental

00:31:21.910 --> 00:31:23.860
to yourself, even
suicidal notions.

00:31:23.860 --> 00:31:27.430
Note that a country like
Israel is Western to the core.

00:31:27.430 --> 00:31:29.410
But it's not as
advanced because it

00:31:29.410 --> 00:31:32.710
knows if it were to experiment
with either pre-modern

00:31:32.710 --> 00:31:35.290
citizenship or
post-modern citizenship,

00:31:35.290 --> 00:31:36.610
its borders would be overrun.

00:31:36.610 --> 00:31:40.540
It would cease to exist because
it lives in a sea of enemies,

00:31:40.540 --> 00:31:42.880
unlike us and most
of Western Europe.

00:31:42.880 --> 00:31:43.760
That's part of it.

00:31:43.760 --> 00:31:46.210
But I would just end by
suggesting that a lot of it

00:31:46.210 --> 00:31:48.190
is our educational system.

00:31:48.190 --> 00:31:53.020
Because the people who are
crafting our laws the judges

00:31:53.020 --> 00:31:57.910
who are overturning plebiscites,
the lawyers who are advancing

00:31:57.910 --> 00:32:00.220
these legal theories,
the professors who

00:32:00.220 --> 00:32:02.680
are teaching our
children all come

00:32:02.680 --> 00:32:06.560
from an asymmetrical system.

00:32:06.560 --> 00:32:10.090
And by that, I mean they
feel that they have reached

00:32:10.090 --> 00:32:14.920
a point of wisdom and affluence
and leisure and comfort,

00:32:14.920 --> 00:32:20.110
and that it's permissible
or, indeed, it's

00:32:20.110 --> 00:32:25.480
desirable or necessary to
explain to people how unhappy,

00:32:25.480 --> 00:32:31.722
how miserable they are, and that
they must change the system.

00:32:31.722 --> 00:32:33.430
And the more that they
change the system,

00:32:33.430 --> 00:32:35.222
you can see what's
happening-- whether it's

00:32:35.222 --> 00:32:40.210
homeless people in San
Francisco juxtaposed to people

00:32:40.210 --> 00:32:42.520
in Nob Hill or Pacific
Heights right next to them

00:32:42.520 --> 00:32:44.240
as they walk across.

00:32:44.240 --> 00:32:47.500
There's an asymmetry,
that we in the university

00:32:47.500 --> 00:32:51.190
live one life, tenured,
affluent, and leisured.

00:32:51.190 --> 00:32:52.810
And we tell indebted
students you

00:32:52.810 --> 00:32:56.770
have to do this with no
ramifications of the ideology

00:32:56.770 --> 00:32:58.603
affecting ourselves.

00:32:58.603 --> 00:33:00.020
And so I think
that we could leave

00:33:00.020 --> 00:33:03.280
by thinking there's
nothing better that we all

00:33:03.280 --> 00:33:06.610
could do than to support
colleges that protect

00:33:06.610 --> 00:33:11.590
the Constitution, they
advocate for the middle class,

00:33:11.590 --> 00:33:15.520
they make a distinction
between residents and citizens,

00:33:15.520 --> 00:33:18.310
and they're not just skeptical,
but they're adamantly

00:33:18.310 --> 00:33:22.120
opposed to anybody who wants
to change the US Constitution--

00:33:22.120 --> 00:33:24.580
the last great hope for
mankind and the only thing that

00:33:24.580 --> 00:33:27.737
saves not just the United
States, but the world at large.

00:33:27.737 --> 00:33:28.570
Thank you very much.

00:33:28.570 --> 00:33:31.522
[APPLAUSE]

00:33:48.533 --> 00:33:49.950
I don't know if
we have questions.

00:33:49.950 --> 00:33:51.150
SPEAKER 2: Thank
you, Dr. Hanson.

00:33:51.150 --> 00:33:52.750
We now have time
for a few questions.

00:33:52.750 --> 00:33:53.970
If you have a question,
please come up

00:33:53.970 --> 00:33:55.070
to one of the microphones.

00:33:58.846 --> 00:34:01.315
AUDIENCE: I have a question.

00:34:01.315 --> 00:34:03.690
VICTOR DAVIS HANSON: I hope
I didn't scare you with that.

00:34:03.690 --> 00:34:04.710
SPEAKER 2: If you
have a question,

00:34:04.710 --> 00:34:06.362
please come up to a microphone.

00:34:06.362 --> 00:34:06.862
OK.

00:34:12.820 --> 00:34:15.340
AUDIENCE: First of all,
thank you very much.

00:34:15.340 --> 00:34:16.130
You were amazing.

00:34:16.130 --> 00:34:20.989
We enjoy you both in your
articles and on television.

00:34:20.989 --> 00:34:23.230
What is your feeling
about the last 10

00:34:23.230 --> 00:34:28.429
days about this so-called
whistleblower issue?

00:34:28.429 --> 00:34:29.929
VICTOR DAVIS HANSON:
So-called what?

00:34:29.929 --> 00:34:31.310
AUDIENCE: Whistleblower issue.

00:34:31.310 --> 00:34:31.969
VICTOR DAVIS HANSON:
Whistleblower issue.

00:34:31.969 --> 00:34:32.960
[INAUDIBLE]

00:34:33.902 --> 00:34:37.199
[LAUGHTER]

00:34:40.050 --> 00:34:41.050
Well, my wife called me.

00:34:41.050 --> 00:34:43.810
She said, what are
you going to speak on?

00:34:43.810 --> 00:34:46.750
And she said, you're not going
to speak on that, are you?

00:34:46.750 --> 00:34:48.864
[LAUGHTER]

00:34:49.593 --> 00:34:51.260
And then she sighed
and said, of course,

00:34:51.260 --> 00:34:53.090
you will be stupid
enough to talk.

00:34:53.090 --> 00:34:54.670
[LAUGHTER]

00:34:54.670 --> 00:35:01.910
Well, very quickly, this latest
psychodrama, it's an a series,

00:35:01.910 --> 00:35:02.660
isn't it?

00:35:02.660 --> 00:35:07.670
The Emoluments Act, the
25th Amendment, Stormy,

00:35:07.670 --> 00:35:09.630
the voting machines, Mueller.

00:35:09.630 --> 00:35:13.310
So it's part of a series
to overturn not just

00:35:13.310 --> 00:35:16.820
the 2016 election date,
but preclude the 2020.

00:35:16.820 --> 00:35:20.120
And it's in a vein of
which I just talked about.

00:35:20.120 --> 00:35:23.850
It's an effort to change the
Constitution, as it were.

00:35:23.850 --> 00:35:25.940
But I think what
Nancy Pelosi has

00:35:25.940 --> 00:35:27.980
done by going forward,
before she even

00:35:27.980 --> 00:35:31.310
had read the transcript of
the call or the whistleblower

00:35:31.310 --> 00:35:35.060
complaint, was
tactically very smart.

00:35:35.060 --> 00:35:38.450
Because Trump has already
gone down 2 points.

00:35:38.450 --> 00:35:40.880
Everybody's forgot
the UN speech he gave.

00:35:40.880 --> 00:35:42.860
Very good speech.

00:35:42.860 --> 00:35:45.710
And then, strategically,
very stupid,

00:35:45.710 --> 00:35:48.200
because it accomplished
all at once

00:35:48.200 --> 00:35:51.410
four or five things that she,
I don't think, anticipated.

00:35:51.410 --> 00:35:55.370
Number one, it took out, for all
practical purposes, the leading

00:35:55.370 --> 00:35:57.470
candidate, Joe Biden.

00:35:57.470 --> 00:36:00.095
And you cannot mention Ukraine--

00:36:00.095 --> 00:36:02.508
you can rail about Trump on
Ukraine, but at some point,

00:36:02.508 --> 00:36:04.550
you have to mention Joe
Biden, even to say, well,

00:36:04.550 --> 00:36:06.030
it doesn't apply to Joe Biden.

00:36:06.030 --> 00:36:06.530
[LAUGHTER]

00:36:06.530 --> 00:36:08.990
That in itself is--

00:36:08.990 --> 00:36:11.420
so I think that he's taken out.

00:36:11.420 --> 00:36:14.900
And he had the best chance
of defeating Donald Trump.

00:36:14.900 --> 00:36:19.350
Second, the whistleblower
and the people

00:36:19.350 --> 00:36:21.210
who put the
whistleblower up-- and I

00:36:21.210 --> 00:36:24.090
say that because I read very
carefully the whistleblower's

00:36:24.090 --> 00:36:27.240
complaint-- it's not written
by a normal whistleblower.

00:36:27.240 --> 00:36:29.370
It's a legal document.

00:36:29.370 --> 00:36:32.790
And two, you cannot impeach the
president of the United States

00:36:32.790 --> 00:36:39.120
based on a whistleblower's
complaint with nine times he

00:36:39.120 --> 00:36:41.460
or she says, I was
told, or I heard.

00:36:41.460 --> 00:36:42.685
It's all hearsay.

00:36:42.685 --> 00:36:44.310
I know the Inspector
General said there

00:36:44.310 --> 00:36:45.780
was some firsthand knowledge.

00:36:45.780 --> 00:36:46.910
I read it four times.

00:36:46.910 --> 00:36:49.710
I couldn't find any
firsthand knowledge.

00:36:49.710 --> 00:36:53.100
And third, you can't impeach the
president on anonymous sources.

00:36:53.100 --> 00:36:55.350
They don't identify
those so-called staffers

00:36:55.350 --> 00:36:57.230
who heard this call.

00:36:57.230 --> 00:36:59.190
And, of course, even if
they did hear the call,

00:36:59.190 --> 00:37:00.982
President of the United
States can bluster,

00:37:00.982 --> 00:37:03.080
cajole, barter, horse--

00:37:03.080 --> 00:37:04.540
That's what they do.

00:37:04.540 --> 00:37:07.140
Can you imagine the phone
call about Barack Obama

00:37:07.140 --> 00:37:10.320
and the nocturnal transfer
of $400 billion to Iran?

00:37:10.320 --> 00:37:11.020
[LAUGHTER]

00:37:11.020 --> 00:37:12.790
I don't want to hear about it.

00:37:12.790 --> 00:37:15.820
But it's his right to do that.

00:37:15.820 --> 00:37:18.750
And so what I'm
getting at is, we're

00:37:18.750 --> 00:37:21.183
going to learn who
the whistleblower is.

00:37:21.183 --> 00:37:23.100
And people are going to
ask the whistleblower,

00:37:23.100 --> 00:37:24.450
have you ever met Adam Schiff?

00:37:24.450 --> 00:37:26.670
And did you ever have a lawyer?

00:37:26.670 --> 00:37:28.740
Did you know that the
whistleblower statute

00:37:28.740 --> 00:37:32.700
was changed to allow hearsay
shortly before you filed this?

00:37:32.700 --> 00:37:35.040
Have you communicated
with anybody in the DNC?

00:37:35.040 --> 00:37:37.950
Those are going to be questions
under penalty of perjury.

00:37:37.950 --> 00:37:41.730
And then, finally, I think Nancy
Pelosi thought that the news

00:37:41.730 --> 00:37:44.730
cycle is predictable.

00:37:44.730 --> 00:37:45.750
You go into impeachment.

00:37:45.750 --> 00:37:46.680
It makes sense.

00:37:46.680 --> 00:37:48.000
Trump might win the election.

00:37:48.000 --> 00:37:48.810
Stop it now.

00:37:48.810 --> 00:37:49.680
But it's not.

00:37:49.680 --> 00:37:51.240
It's volatile.

00:37:51.240 --> 00:37:54.670
And by that, I mean there's
too many known unknowns.

00:37:54.670 --> 00:37:57.840
There's Michael Horowitz with
maybe a criminal indictment

00:37:57.840 --> 00:37:59.710
of Andrew McCabe.

00:37:59.710 --> 00:38:01.820
There's Michael
Horowitz maybe you

00:38:01.820 --> 00:38:05.070
with a criminal indictment about
the abuse of the FISA court.

00:38:05.070 --> 00:38:06.540
There's John
Huebner, who's going

00:38:06.540 --> 00:38:09.210
all over the world trying
to find who interfered

00:38:09.210 --> 00:38:12.070
against Trump, not for Trump.

00:38:12.070 --> 00:38:16.090
And then you have John Durham,
who's doing the same thing.

00:38:16.090 --> 00:38:18.675
So you have three prosecutors
who are looking at things

00:38:18.675 --> 00:38:19.800
that we've never looked at.

00:38:19.800 --> 00:38:22.380
Who knows what the
news will be like that?

00:38:22.380 --> 00:38:24.360
We've only had 3
of the-- was it 12?

00:38:24.360 --> 00:38:25.830
We have 9 more of these debates.

00:38:25.830 --> 00:38:27.330
I mean, every time
there's a debate,

00:38:27.330 --> 00:38:28.710
Trump goes up by 2 points.

00:38:28.710 --> 00:38:30.496
[LAUGHTER]

00:38:30.496 --> 00:38:33.244
[APPLAUSE]

00:38:34.620 --> 00:38:38.110
And so what I'm getting at is
that going after and trying

00:38:38.110 --> 00:38:41.650
to impeach a president
on anonymous sources

00:38:41.650 --> 00:38:45.850
and hearsay while you have
three prosecutors loose

00:38:45.850 --> 00:38:48.670
and you have debates going
on with the New Green Deal

00:38:48.670 --> 00:38:52.690
advocacy and reparation, it's
not a good formula, I think.

00:38:52.690 --> 00:38:56.350
And I think the result will
be, after about three weeks,

00:38:56.350 --> 00:38:57.650
she'll start to see that.

00:38:57.650 --> 00:39:00.850
And then it'll be an
impeachment inquiry and not

00:39:00.850 --> 00:39:02.470
a true impeachment.

00:39:02.470 --> 00:39:05.170
If they were to vote on it-- and
I think Republicans very much

00:39:05.170 --> 00:39:07.650
want them to vote--

00:39:07.650 --> 00:39:11.170
30 or 40 House Representatives
were going to lose their seats.

00:39:11.170 --> 00:39:15.310
Because where we are in
California, my district,

00:39:15.310 --> 00:39:18.790
our Republican candidate
lost by 600 votes.

00:39:18.790 --> 00:39:21.130
He won the election by 6,000.

00:39:21.130 --> 00:39:24.610
And then voter harvesting by
Christmas, he lost by 600.

00:39:24.610 --> 00:39:25.960
He's running again.

00:39:25.960 --> 00:39:29.020
And according to the McClatchy
poll, he's 12 points ahead.

00:39:29.020 --> 00:39:31.480
And he's really
hammering this against

00:39:31.480 --> 00:39:32.933
the Democratic incumbent.

00:39:32.933 --> 00:39:34.350
Thank you very
much, the question.

00:39:34.350 --> 00:39:37.682
[APPLAUSE]

00:39:41.500 --> 00:39:45.090
AUDIENCE: Dr. Hanson, it's
a privilege to see you.

00:39:45.090 --> 00:39:49.500
I left Los Banos, California,
about six years ago.

00:39:49.500 --> 00:39:55.410
And when I left a city of
35,000, the Ford dealership--

00:39:55.410 --> 00:39:57.750
because I had a Mustang--

00:39:57.750 --> 00:40:00.030
was down to four mechanics.

00:40:00.030 --> 00:40:02.940
And they all left at noon.

00:40:02.940 --> 00:40:07.080
You said in your book,
Mexifornia, that you wrote,

00:40:07.080 --> 00:40:12.360
I believe, in 2003, you said,
"This state cannot figure out

00:40:12.360 --> 00:40:16.410
whether it has become a promised
land based on cheap immigrant

00:40:16.410 --> 00:40:23.780
labor or a looming nightmare of
unassimilated Third Worldism."

00:40:23.780 --> 00:40:25.530
Now the reason I think
that's so critical,

00:40:25.530 --> 00:40:29.400
I'd like to have more
comment on that from you

00:40:29.400 --> 00:40:32.850
is because, as you say in
your book, what's happening

00:40:32.850 --> 00:40:35.410
there is coming
to the rest of us.

00:40:38.686 --> 00:40:41.026
[SCATTERED LAUGHTER]

00:40:41.260 --> 00:40:43.010
VICTOR DAVIS HANSON:
That was a funny book

00:40:43.010 --> 00:40:45.635
because the person who asked me
to write the article from which

00:40:45.635 --> 00:40:48.530
the book came 20 years
ago said, can you

00:40:48.530 --> 00:40:50.810
write how wonderful
illegal immigration is?

00:40:50.810 --> 00:40:52.640
[LAUGHTER]

00:40:52.640 --> 00:40:54.032
And I said, I can
write about it.

00:40:54.032 --> 00:40:55.115
But it won't be wonderful.

00:40:55.115 --> 00:40:56.160
[LAUGHTER]

00:40:56.160 --> 00:41:01.590
So I think what we saw in
California in 2002 and 2003,

00:41:01.590 --> 00:41:04.860
when I wrote that, is
happening all over the country.

00:41:04.860 --> 00:41:11.570
And what changed--
and by that, I mean,

00:41:11.570 --> 00:41:13.903
I guess in our business, we
all have this blood sport

00:41:13.903 --> 00:41:15.820
where we go back and
look at YouTube comments.

00:41:15.820 --> 00:41:18.140
Have you seen John
Kerry, Hillary Clinton,

00:41:18.140 --> 00:41:21.092
Bill Clinton blasting
illegal immigration?

00:41:21.092 --> 00:41:21.800
You should do it.

00:41:21.800 --> 00:41:24.290
There's a great one
with Barack Obama.

00:41:24.290 --> 00:41:25.790
He says, I'm not a king.

00:41:25.790 --> 00:41:27.410
I can't give amnesty.

00:41:27.410 --> 00:41:29.300
22 times he said that.

00:41:29.300 --> 00:41:30.410
So what changed?

00:41:30.410 --> 00:41:33.470
What changed is that people said
that in the Democratic Party

00:41:33.470 --> 00:41:36.020
when there were 2,
4, and 6 million.

00:41:36.020 --> 00:41:39.530
But when you have 20 million
and you have a second generation

00:41:39.530 --> 00:41:42.980
already that's at voting age
of another 10 or 15 million

00:41:42.980 --> 00:41:46.370
of US citizens, then that's
a powerful constituency.

00:41:46.370 --> 00:41:49.010
And they look at
California, and they say,

00:41:49.010 --> 00:41:53.750
wow, this was 32
years of Reagan,

00:41:53.750 --> 00:41:56.690
Pete Wilson, Deukmejian,
Schwarzenegger-- never going

00:41:56.690 --> 00:41:57.950
to happen again.

00:41:57.950 --> 00:41:59.810
Supermajority in
both legislatures.

00:41:59.810 --> 00:42:03.020
We went down from 53
congressional seats

00:42:03.020 --> 00:42:05.150
to seven Republican.

00:42:05.150 --> 00:42:08.690
So they look at Nevada, flipped.

00:42:08.690 --> 00:42:10.430
New Mexico's flipped blue.

00:42:10.430 --> 00:42:13.410
Texas and Arizona and
Colorado are next.

00:42:13.410 --> 00:42:14.850
And that's a demographic shift.

00:42:14.850 --> 00:42:18.080
So the Democratic Party
is for open borders

00:42:18.080 --> 00:42:22.070
because they find
a lot of advantage.

00:42:22.070 --> 00:42:24.710
The only thing that's
going to save us

00:42:24.710 --> 00:42:26.810
is what we talked
about-- citizenship,

00:42:26.810 --> 00:42:31.040
that we appeal to people who
are coming hopefully legally,

00:42:31.040 --> 00:42:34.970
hopefully with skills, hopefully
through a meritocratic process,

00:42:34.970 --> 00:42:37.330
hopefully in a diverse
manner so they're

00:42:37.330 --> 00:42:38.330
from all over the world.

00:42:38.330 --> 00:42:41.120
And we say to them,
you left your country.

00:42:41.120 --> 00:42:42.230
You don't like Oaxaca.

00:42:42.230 --> 00:42:44.240
You may think you like Oaxaca.

00:42:44.240 --> 00:42:45.920
You may put a Mexican
flag on your car.

00:42:45.920 --> 00:42:46.590
But you're here.

00:42:46.590 --> 00:42:48.950
So that tells me you
did not like it there.

00:42:48.950 --> 00:42:50.668
So we're here to help you.

00:42:50.668 --> 00:42:53.210
We're here to integrate you and
assimilate you and intermarry

00:42:53.210 --> 00:42:55.010
you and become one of us.

00:42:55.010 --> 00:42:58.220
And if Trump can
make that argument--

00:42:58.220 --> 00:43:00.780
and he can also make
the economic argument.

00:43:00.780 --> 00:43:03.710
He could say, I've got
the lowest unemployment

00:43:03.710 --> 00:43:05.840
in the history of the
Mexican-American workforce.

00:43:05.840 --> 00:43:07.220
It's true.

00:43:07.220 --> 00:43:09.470
And for someone who
lives in that area,

00:43:09.470 --> 00:43:13.130
it's just absolutely astounding
to hear people come up to you

00:43:13.130 --> 00:43:17.620
and say, I was on the roof.

00:43:17.620 --> 00:43:19.710
They're paying me $19 an hour.

00:43:19.710 --> 00:43:21.360
For the first time
in my life, Victor,

00:43:21.360 --> 00:43:23.740
a guy came over and got
out of his car and said,

00:43:23.740 --> 00:43:26.480
I'll pay you $23 if you get off
that roof and go on my road.

00:43:26.480 --> 00:43:26.980
[LAUGHTER]

00:43:26.980 --> 00:43:28.690
And he said, they want me now.

00:43:28.690 --> 00:43:31.020
And so what we forgot
in the Republican Party,

00:43:31.020 --> 00:43:33.090
are that the essence
of dignity is a job.

00:43:33.090 --> 00:43:36.600
And when you get the
unemployment rate down to 3.7%

00:43:36.600 --> 00:43:38.700
and you have employers
begging for workers,

00:43:38.700 --> 00:43:42.450
that worker calculates that
and thinks, I have dignity now.

00:43:42.450 --> 00:43:45.040
I have leverage.

00:43:45.040 --> 00:43:49.200
I have some gratitude to
the person who did that.

00:43:49.200 --> 00:43:53.850
So wouldn't that be ironic,
that through economics

00:43:53.850 --> 00:43:56.520
and a new approach to
Mexican-American voters,

00:43:56.520 --> 00:44:00.220
one based on citizenship
and "become one of us,"

00:44:00.220 --> 00:44:05.560
the Democratic Party would
lose what they need to win.

00:44:05.560 --> 00:44:09.010
And because they've so
alienated the working classes,

00:44:09.010 --> 00:44:10.840
especially the
white working class,

00:44:10.840 --> 00:44:13.290
they cannot afford to lose
40% of the Hispanic vote,

00:44:13.290 --> 00:44:16.110
or even 15% of the black vote.

00:44:16.110 --> 00:44:20.216
And yet, I think there's a
good chance they'll do both.

00:44:20.216 --> 00:44:23.604
[APPLAUSE]

00:44:27.808 --> 00:44:28.600
AUDIENCE: Question.

00:44:28.600 --> 00:44:32.790
Since your book,
Carnage and Culture,

00:44:32.790 --> 00:44:35.910
and what's happened today,
if you were to go back

00:44:35.910 --> 00:44:37.920
or to look at what
you wrote then

00:44:37.920 --> 00:44:41.730
and what is happening now,
what are the underpinnings,

00:44:41.730 --> 00:44:46.500
in your opinion, being a
student of culture that gives us

00:44:46.500 --> 00:44:52.680
a foundation or perhaps a
way or a path back to culture

00:44:52.680 --> 00:44:57.065
that will remain as strong
as our Constitution intended?

00:45:03.212 --> 00:45:04.670
VICTOR DAVIS HANSON:
Well, when you

00:45:04.670 --> 00:45:08.840
say the West, Western culture,
really we have to be specific.

00:45:08.840 --> 00:45:10.620
We're talking about
North America--

00:45:10.620 --> 00:45:12.500
Canada, and the United States.

00:45:12.500 --> 00:45:17.780
And we're talking about
Britain and its former empire

00:45:17.780 --> 00:45:18.920
in most part--

00:45:18.920 --> 00:45:20.940
Australia, Canada, New Zealand.

00:45:20.940 --> 00:45:23.180
And we're talking about
continental Europe.

00:45:23.180 --> 00:45:25.790
And then we're now talking
about westernized states,

00:45:25.790 --> 00:45:29.210
such as South Korea or Taiwan
or Japan that have more

00:45:29.210 --> 00:45:31.220
or less accepted the paradigm.

00:45:31.220 --> 00:45:34.460
And out of 7 billion people,
there's about 2 billion

00:45:34.460 --> 00:45:36.420
that are on that page.

00:45:36.420 --> 00:45:38.070
And so that's helpful.

00:45:38.070 --> 00:45:43.730
But what is worrying to me
is that while globalization

00:45:43.730 --> 00:45:46.490
and westernization
has swept the globe.

00:45:46.490 --> 00:45:49.940
So if I go to the Ukraine
or I go to Bolivia,

00:45:49.940 --> 00:45:52.430
people have jeans,
they have a cell phone,

00:45:52.430 --> 00:45:56.180
they're on Facebook, they seem
they're almost quasi-Americans.

00:45:56.180 --> 00:45:59.330
That diffusion has
really weakened the West.

00:45:59.330 --> 00:46:02.000
And by that, I mean
people think the West is

00:46:02.000 --> 00:46:04.110
that X-Men movie I saw.

00:46:04.110 --> 00:46:07.700
Or the West is that
cell phone, as I said.

00:46:07.700 --> 00:46:09.860
Or it's that
particular rap song.

00:46:09.860 --> 00:46:11.150
It's not.

00:46:11.150 --> 00:46:16.520
It's a menu of constitutional
government, free speech, due

00:46:16.520 --> 00:46:20.060
process, the protection
of private property,

00:46:20.060 --> 00:46:21.125
the right to bear arms.

00:46:24.440 --> 00:46:30.130
I think I don't want to be
too fanatical or too paranoid.

00:46:30.130 --> 00:46:32.480
But I think that menu is
almost gone in Europe,

00:46:32.480 --> 00:46:34.470
as I see it today.

00:46:34.470 --> 00:46:36.550
And you can see what's
happening in Brexit today

00:46:36.550 --> 00:46:38.530
when people had a plebiscite.

00:46:38.530 --> 00:46:41.170
We do not want to be part
of this continental system.

00:46:41.170 --> 00:46:43.100
It's different
than, we in Britain.

00:46:43.100 --> 00:46:45.890
It's not the Anglo-Saxon,
Anglo-American tradition,

00:46:45.890 --> 00:46:47.830
the transatlantic.

00:46:47.830 --> 00:46:48.590
It's different.

00:46:48.590 --> 00:46:50.080
We don't want it anymore.

00:46:50.080 --> 00:46:51.335
It's anti-democratic.

00:46:51.335 --> 00:46:52.960
It's against the
traditions of Britain.

00:46:52.960 --> 00:46:56.320
And they can't get out,
because their own elites have

00:46:56.320 --> 00:46:57.700
joined the continental system.

00:46:57.700 --> 00:46:59.980
So what I most
worried about when

00:46:59.980 --> 00:47:01.420
I wrote about
Carnage And Culture,

00:47:01.420 --> 00:47:03.400
I use that word the "West."

00:47:03.400 --> 00:47:07.180
And I know that there's been
bifurcations during world wars.

00:47:07.180 --> 00:47:10.660
But what's really scary is that
we're going to an Orwellian

00:47:10.660 --> 00:47:12.730
sameness in the West.

00:47:12.730 --> 00:47:18.610
And the United States is
not completely against it.

00:47:18.610 --> 00:47:22.120
Half of the United States
agrees with the trajectory

00:47:22.120 --> 00:47:23.650
that Europe is in.

00:47:23.650 --> 00:47:25.870
And they like the
European Union.

00:47:25.870 --> 00:47:28.180
And they like the idea
that somebody in Brussels

00:47:28.180 --> 00:47:30.970
can tell a Greek that that's
not a banana because it's not

00:47:30.970 --> 00:47:33.880
6 inches long, or you
can't go to your beach

00:47:33.880 --> 00:47:36.170
because one of our inspectors
saw plastic bottles,

00:47:36.170 --> 00:47:37.640
so we shut it down.

00:47:37.640 --> 00:47:39.610
They liked that
control, because they

00:47:39.610 --> 00:47:45.585
feel that a special educated,
affluent class, mostly

00:47:45.585 --> 00:47:46.960
on the coast of
the United States

00:47:46.960 --> 00:47:49.510
and Europe in the
big cities, knows

00:47:49.510 --> 00:47:53.050
far better than the average
person what's good for them.

00:47:53.050 --> 00:47:54.880
And to the degree
that they're wrong,

00:47:54.880 --> 00:47:57.610
they have a parachute by their
own influence and wealth.

00:47:57.610 --> 00:48:01.778
But their bromides will never
apply to them, themselves.

00:48:01.778 --> 00:48:03.820
And that's what's really
scary because the United

00:48:03.820 --> 00:48:08.120
States was built on this middle
class "match word with deed."

00:48:08.120 --> 00:48:14.570
And my mother was the first
female superior court judge

00:48:14.570 --> 00:48:18.760
in Fresno County, and the second
female appellate court judge.

00:48:18.760 --> 00:48:22.270
And I had a grandfather
that mortgaged his ranch

00:48:22.270 --> 00:48:23.320
with very little money--

00:48:23.320 --> 00:48:27.010
little, tiny farm-- and
put all his children

00:48:27.010 --> 00:48:29.358
to college, and
two, in the 1940s,

00:48:29.358 --> 00:48:31.150
for graduate and
undergraduate at Stanford.

00:48:31.150 --> 00:48:33.820
And I remember her going
to the farmer's market

00:48:33.820 --> 00:48:36.730
when she was an appellate
court judge, peddling

00:48:36.730 --> 00:48:40.490
pears and plums with us
on Saturdays, out there

00:48:40.490 --> 00:48:41.590
without an apron on.

00:48:41.590 --> 00:48:44.542
And I'd say, well,
wow, Judge Hanson's

00:48:44.542 --> 00:48:46.000
out here-- making
fun of my mother.

00:48:46.000 --> 00:48:47.680
And she said, it's
very, very important

00:48:47.680 --> 00:48:51.610
to remember that in America,
the elite get their hands dirty.

00:48:51.610 --> 00:48:53.470
And I'm elite only in title.

00:48:53.470 --> 00:48:54.430
I'm not part of that.

00:48:54.430 --> 00:48:56.140
And that was very
important that we

00:48:56.140 --> 00:48:59.500
realize that physical
work has dignity

00:48:59.500 --> 00:49:01.630
and that people who
work with their hands

00:49:01.630 --> 00:49:05.870
and are in the middle class are
the bulwark of this country.

00:49:05.870 --> 00:49:09.700
They have a common sense that
is invaluable to this country.

00:49:09.700 --> 00:49:14.080
We almost forgot
that until 2016.

00:49:14.080 --> 00:49:16.580
And I'm not going
to get political.

00:49:16.580 --> 00:49:18.190
But when Trump,
for the first time,

00:49:18.190 --> 00:49:20.710
said our workers,
our vets, our--

00:49:20.710 --> 00:49:23.107
I like the word "our."

00:49:23.107 --> 00:49:24.190
I mean, he was ridiculous.

00:49:24.190 --> 00:49:26.090
He went to West Virginia.

00:49:26.090 --> 00:49:29.188
And Hillary had said, we're
going to shut you all down.

00:49:29.188 --> 00:49:31.480
You're going to have to make
solar panels, essentially.

00:49:31.480 --> 00:49:33.630
And he said, I love
big, beautiful coal.

00:49:33.630 --> 00:49:36.040
[LAUGHTER]

00:49:36.040 --> 00:49:40.870
What I'm getting at is
that I don't see that.

00:49:40.870 --> 00:49:45.310
I see a Marxist paradigm between
workers and then capitalists

00:49:45.310 --> 00:49:45.810
in Europe.

00:49:45.810 --> 00:49:48.270
But what I see in
America was always

00:49:48.270 --> 00:49:50.100
a reverence for
the working class,

00:49:50.100 --> 00:49:52.190
just right from Jefferson
from the beginning

00:49:52.190 --> 00:49:53.160
of the Constitution.

00:49:53.160 --> 00:49:57.450
And we've really got to get
back to the idea that none of us

00:49:57.450 --> 00:49:59.520
are above the idea
of physical work.

00:49:59.520 --> 00:50:04.313
When you have somebody build
your roof or mow your lawn,

00:50:04.313 --> 00:50:06.480
that's just as important
as what we're doing up here

00:50:06.480 --> 00:50:07.087
in this stage.

00:50:07.087 --> 00:50:08.670
And we really have
to get that dignity

00:50:08.670 --> 00:50:10.950
and respect back
for muscular labor.

00:50:10.950 --> 00:50:14.760
In this outsourcing,
offshoring, globalist economy,

00:50:14.760 --> 00:50:15.790
we forgot that.

00:50:15.790 --> 00:50:18.290
And I think we've been
saved at the last moment.

00:50:18.290 --> 00:50:18.790
Thank you.

00:50:18.790 --> 00:50:22.136
[APPLAUSE]

00:50:27.890 --> 00:50:30.770
AUDIENCE: Should we
invite 1,000 Venezuelans

00:50:30.770 --> 00:50:33.430
to live in each one of our
congressional districts

00:50:33.430 --> 00:50:34.785
to remind us of the alternative?

00:50:34.785 --> 00:50:36.410
SPEAKER 3: Should we
invite Venezuelans

00:50:36.410 --> 00:50:38.260
to live in our
congressional districts

00:50:38.260 --> 00:50:39.591
to remind us about socialism?

00:50:39.591 --> 00:50:42.477
[LAUGHTER]

00:50:45.850 --> 00:50:48.580
VICTOR DAVIS HANSON: Did
you hear the question?

00:50:48.580 --> 00:50:49.730
I think we don't need them.

00:50:49.730 --> 00:50:50.830
We have enough professors.

00:50:50.830 --> 00:50:54.127
[LAUGHTER]

00:50:58.460 --> 00:51:00.070
I don't know how we are on time.

00:51:12.350 --> 00:51:16.620
AUDIENCE: I very much enjoyed
your speech or whatever--

00:51:16.620 --> 00:51:18.380
presentation.

00:51:18.380 --> 00:51:24.770
You mentioned that the idea
of maybe the senators having

00:51:24.770 --> 00:51:28.700
the same representation
as representatives.

00:51:28.700 --> 00:51:31.670
Now, about six to eight months
ago, there was a writer--

00:51:31.670 --> 00:51:35.850
and I think it was the Wall
Street Journal-- that predicted

00:51:35.850 --> 00:51:40.280
that the Democrats would have
a difficult time in the future

00:51:40.280 --> 00:51:42.320
ever getting the Senate back.

00:51:42.320 --> 00:51:44.440
And I don't remember the logic.

00:51:44.440 --> 00:51:47.300
And I was wondering if you
would read that article

00:51:47.300 --> 00:51:50.772
or knew why he made
that assertion.

00:51:57.207 --> 00:51:59.790
VICTOR DAVIS HANSON: I mean, we
don't want to be philosophers,

00:51:59.790 --> 00:52:04.020
but there is a mentality behind
all this if you think about it.

00:52:04.020 --> 00:52:12.960
So the Democrats, when
they're in the majority,

00:52:12.960 --> 00:52:14.860
they don't want filibusters.

00:52:14.860 --> 00:52:16.930
Filibusters are
archaic and disruptive.

00:52:16.930 --> 00:52:20.080
When they're in the minority,
they want to bring them back.

00:52:20.080 --> 00:52:20.890
Believe me.

00:52:20.890 --> 00:52:26.650
If Donald Trump wins a popular
vote and Elizabeth Warren

00:52:26.650 --> 00:52:28.930
wins the electoral college,
the electoral college

00:52:28.930 --> 00:52:30.850
will be the greatest
thing since sliced bread.

00:52:30.850 --> 00:52:31.525
[LAUGHTER]

00:52:31.525 --> 00:52:32.900
And the same thing
with senators.

00:52:32.900 --> 00:52:39.578
If tomorrow we have 100
Democratic senators,

00:52:39.578 --> 00:52:41.620
and they're going to say,
this is the best system

00:52:41.620 --> 00:52:43.240
the Founders ever came up on.

00:52:43.240 --> 00:52:45.790
So what I'm getting at
is that I don't really

00:52:45.790 --> 00:52:47.080
take the Left at its word.

00:52:47.080 --> 00:52:49.540
I don't think they have a
consistent philosophy, other

00:52:49.540 --> 00:52:54.330
than we believe in
equality of result.

00:52:54.330 --> 00:52:57.520
We're morally superior because
we have greater empathy.

00:52:57.520 --> 00:53:00.820
And therefore, because we're
morally superior creatures

00:53:00.820 --> 00:53:05.080
to you, any means necessary,
any inconsistencies

00:53:05.080 --> 00:53:09.280
is allowable because our
aims are so much greater

00:53:09.280 --> 00:53:14.020
than your greedy, individualist,
profit-mongering, selfishness.

00:53:14.020 --> 00:53:16.000
And that's how they operate.

00:53:16.000 --> 00:53:20.500
And therefore, when we look
at this impeachment circus--

00:53:20.500 --> 00:53:23.410
and I'm talking about
people in my own family--

00:53:23.410 --> 00:53:26.110
when you talk to them,
the details don't matter.

00:53:26.110 --> 00:53:27.820
What all that matters
is these are people

00:53:27.820 --> 00:53:29.650
who are out to help people.

00:53:29.650 --> 00:53:32.560
And they're trying to oppose
people who are selfish.

00:53:32.560 --> 00:53:35.900
And therefore, anything
that goes on is justified.

00:53:35.900 --> 00:53:40.180
And that's been the story
of Progressivism and Leftism

00:53:40.180 --> 00:53:42.548
since antiquity.

00:53:42.548 --> 00:53:44.465
SPEAKER 2: We have time
for one more question.

00:53:53.950 --> 00:53:56.090
AUDIENCE: Thank you very much.

00:53:56.090 --> 00:53:58.000
You've been a historian
and studied history

00:53:58.000 --> 00:53:59.980
over the millennia--
not your millennia,

00:53:59.980 --> 00:54:01.690
but the world's millennia.

00:54:01.690 --> 00:54:03.130
[LAUGHTER]

00:54:04.340 --> 00:54:06.250
Is there any other
comparable time

00:54:06.250 --> 00:54:09.430
in other civilizations that
went through what we're going

00:54:09.430 --> 00:54:12.430
through now, and their outcome?

00:54:12.430 --> 00:54:15.670
And how would you foresee
the United States in 10 years

00:54:15.670 --> 00:54:18.142
compared to how other
civilizations have

00:54:18.142 --> 00:54:19.225
worked out these problems?

00:54:23.270 --> 00:54:26.390
VICTOR DAVIS HANSON:
Well, I think

00:54:26.390 --> 00:54:29.660
the greatest treatise that's
ever been written on politics

00:54:29.660 --> 00:54:34.010
is Aristotle's
Politics, because he

00:54:34.010 --> 00:54:37.680
transcends the particular
case studies that he educes.

00:54:37.680 --> 00:54:39.800
And what he just
assumes is, there's

00:54:39.800 --> 00:54:43.880
always going to be a class
struggle or political struggle

00:54:43.880 --> 00:54:46.620
between different groups.

00:54:46.620 --> 00:54:50.120
And if I could reduce
that to a banality

00:54:50.120 --> 00:54:52.580
it's, there's some
people who believe in

00:54:52.580 --> 00:54:56.870
a equality of opportunity
and some believe

00:54:56.870 --> 00:54:59.300
in a state-mandated
equality of result.

00:54:59.300 --> 00:55:01.910
The people who believe in
the equality of opportunity

00:55:01.910 --> 00:55:03.440
say this.

00:55:03.440 --> 00:55:07.100
Unfortunately, we
weren't all born equally.

00:55:07.100 --> 00:55:08.810
Some of us have more talent.

00:55:08.810 --> 00:55:10.310
Some of us have better health.

00:55:10.310 --> 00:55:11.570
Some of us have better luck.

00:55:11.570 --> 00:55:13.580
Some of us had a
rich grandfather.

00:55:13.580 --> 00:55:15.290
Life is unfair.

00:55:15.290 --> 00:55:17.300
But let us go and do our thing.

00:55:17.300 --> 00:55:19.190
And then we will have
social pressures,

00:55:19.190 --> 00:55:22.490
like you people,
here, to give back.

00:55:22.490 --> 00:55:23.750
Let me go make a lot of money.

00:55:23.750 --> 00:55:26.540
And then let me choose to
help support Hillsdale.

00:55:26.540 --> 00:55:27.690
I will do that.

00:55:27.690 --> 00:55:28.760
I'm not a greedy person.

00:55:28.760 --> 00:55:31.340
There's only so hot
that my shower can get.

00:55:31.340 --> 00:55:33.780
And my BMW only go so fast.

00:55:33.780 --> 00:55:35.700
I can't spend all that
I'm going to make.

00:55:35.700 --> 00:55:38.880
But I want to do it for
a variety of reasons.

00:55:38.880 --> 00:55:41.090
And that group is
usually attacked

00:55:41.090 --> 00:55:43.560
by people for various reasons.

00:55:43.560 --> 00:55:46.490
And Plato and Aristotle
were no dummies.

00:55:46.490 --> 00:55:49.160
They talk about
psychological reasons--

00:55:49.160 --> 00:55:52.340
that people get envious.

00:55:52.340 --> 00:55:55.220
Envy and jealousy, Hesiod
said, are the two most

00:55:55.220 --> 00:55:56.900
powerful emotions we have.

00:55:56.900 --> 00:55:59.690
The other people say, we
can't trust those people.

00:55:59.690 --> 00:56:02.870
They don't give in a way
that's rational or moral.

00:56:02.870 --> 00:56:05.540
So we want to take
what they have

00:56:05.540 --> 00:56:08.150
and distribute it to
the more noble people.

00:56:08.150 --> 00:56:11.990
And that dichotomy is the
story of Western civilization.

00:56:11.990 --> 00:56:16.680
And when it works, we keep
it within the boundaries.

00:56:16.680 --> 00:56:18.270
And we have certain
ground rules.

00:56:18.270 --> 00:56:21.200
You shall not
resort to violence.

00:56:21.200 --> 00:56:25.490
You shall relegate politics
to a secondary level.

00:56:25.490 --> 00:56:28.790
And you will absorb it
with nationalist pride

00:56:28.790 --> 00:56:31.250
and collective patriotism.

00:56:31.250 --> 00:56:35.480
But every once in a while,
Rome in the first century, when

00:56:35.480 --> 00:56:40.070
we had a terrible civil war,
or the stasis at Corcyra

00:56:40.070 --> 00:56:46.730
in Thucydides, or two or three
times before the Civil War,

00:56:46.730 --> 00:56:50.690
but especially during the US
Civil War, or during the '60s.

00:56:50.690 --> 00:56:53.300
I can remember being at UC,
Santa Cruz, in a lecture

00:56:53.300 --> 00:56:55.700
just like this in 1971.

00:56:55.700 --> 00:56:58.810
And Jasper Rose, a distinguished
art historian was lecturing.

00:56:58.810 --> 00:57:03.710
And two people ran up
and tore the exhibits

00:57:03.710 --> 00:57:06.680
he had down and stepped
on them and said, F

00:57:06.680 --> 00:57:08.360
your bourgeoisie art.

00:57:08.360 --> 00:57:10.287
At certain times,
we've had these people

00:57:10.287 --> 00:57:11.870
who did not want to
play by the rules,

00:57:11.870 --> 00:57:13.710
and sometimes on both sides.

00:57:13.710 --> 00:57:16.490
And it's incumbent
upon all of us to say,

00:57:16.490 --> 00:57:20.720
whatever our disagreements
are about this ancient divide,

00:57:20.720 --> 00:57:23.240
we want to instruct
you why you're wrong.

00:57:23.240 --> 00:57:26.990
And we want to do it
through persuasion and love.

00:57:26.990 --> 00:57:30.930
And the people who don't want
to do that, unfortunately,

00:57:30.930 --> 00:57:32.900
are going to have
to pay a price.

00:57:32.900 --> 00:57:35.240
And human nature
being what it is,

00:57:35.240 --> 00:57:37.610
most people don't have
very much courage.

00:57:37.610 --> 00:57:42.230
So believe me, if we had
courageous university

00:57:42.230 --> 00:57:47.630
presidents outside of Hillsdale,
and we had a courageous police

00:57:47.630 --> 00:57:50.690
chief-- and the first time
an Antifa protester went up

00:57:50.690 --> 00:57:52.850
on stage and hit somebody--

00:57:52.850 --> 00:57:54.710
these people are not
rebels, believe me.

00:57:54.710 --> 00:57:57.617
They're upper middle-class
kids that have all the career

00:57:57.617 --> 00:57:58.700
ambitions of anybody else.

00:57:58.700 --> 00:58:00.770
If you charge him
with a felony assault

00:58:00.770 --> 00:58:03.100
and that was on his record,
that would be the last time

00:58:03.100 --> 00:58:04.280
he ever did that.

00:58:04.280 --> 00:58:05.810
There is such a
thing as deterrence.

00:58:05.810 --> 00:58:09.830
But what we've had now is, we've
allowed this ancient debate

00:58:09.830 --> 00:58:12.620
to get way beyond
the old parameters.

00:58:12.620 --> 00:58:14.450
And it's starting
to get violent.

00:58:14.450 --> 00:58:15.830
And it's starting to get absurd.

00:58:15.830 --> 00:58:17.122
And it's starting to get nasty.

00:58:17.122 --> 00:58:20.960
Because we don't have people
who are courageous enough

00:58:20.960 --> 00:58:23.490
to step in and say that you're
not going to do that anymore.

00:58:23.490 --> 00:58:25.490
There is a rule of law
and the Constitution.

00:58:25.490 --> 00:58:29.440
Until we return to that, it's
going to spiral out of control.

00:58:29.440 --> 00:58:31.730
And we just need one
or two honest people

00:58:31.730 --> 00:58:33.710
to stand up and say, no more.

00:58:33.710 --> 00:58:35.007
So far, we haven't found them.

00:58:35.007 --> 00:58:35.840
Thank you very much.

00:58:35.840 --> 00:58:37.640
[APPLAUSE]

00:58:58.040 --> 00:59:01.390
[MUSIC PLAYING]

